---
/**
 * hospitality / CredentialsBK — "Why you can trust the booking" (Villa Marindu).
 *
 * ROLE: the design's CREDENTIALS band — an eyebrow, one display headline, and
 * below it a single hairline-boxed RAIL split into four equal cells. Each cell
 * opens with a brass serif numeral, then an 11px tracked-caps title, then a
 * 13px sentence. It is the owner's standing on record (owner-managed, an
 * accreditation, written terms), which is why every cell is owner-typed and
 * nothing in it is drawn by this file.
 *
 * DESIGN FIDELITY — every value is lifted from the CREDENTIALS element of
 * "Villa Marindu Landing.dc.html", mapped hex → token (R1):
 *   eyebrow  10px / .44em / 600 / #B99552 ........ the wrapper's .kick
 *   headline clamp(31px,4vw,54px) / 1.1 / #F4F1EA  the wrapper's .h2, margin
 *            .26em top, 40px to the rail
 *   rail     4 × minmax(0,1fr), 1px #262E2B box, 3px radius, overflow hidden
 *            → repeat(4,...), var(--rule), var(--r-lg)
 *   cell     34px 26px on #141A18 with a 1px #262E2B divider
 *            → var(--night-2), var(--rule)
 *   numeral  Prata 22px #B99552 + 14px  → the wrapper's .fig, var(--accent)
 *   title    11px / .2em / 600 / #F4F1EA → var(--sans), var(--bone-hi)
 *   body     13px / 1.7 / #767C76        → var(--muted)
 * No literal hex anywhere; the accent is the only token an admin scheme moves.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (all four are declared in genericContentSchema.ts, section
 * id 'credentials' — nothing here is a dead hook):
 *   credentials.tag                — the eyebrow
 *   credentials.headline           — the h2, set:html so an owner can put the
 *                                    design's italic brass <em> anywhere in
 *                                    their own sentence
 *   credentials.items.${i}.title   — the tracked-caps cell title
 *   credentials.items.${i}.body    — the sentence under it. CANONICAL key is
 *                                    `body`: the sidebar writes rows as
 *                                    {title, body} and astro-builder's
 *                                    normalizeBlock COPIES body into desc
 *                                    (never deletes it), so the value reads
 *                                    from either — but the hook must spell
 *                                    `body`, which is what the schema declares.
 *                                    CredentialsBI spells `desc`; that hook is
 *                                    dead. Do not copy it.
 *
 * DERIVED, and therefore carrying NO data-field:
 *   · the numeral (01, 02, 03…) is built from the DISPLAY index, so the rail
 *     always counts without gaps however many blank rows were dropped, and an
 *     edit can never be misdirected into a counter. aria-hidden: it is chrome.
 *   · the cell count: the rail is the design's 4 tracks when there are ≥4 rows,
 *     and exactly rows.length tracks below that, so two credentials fill the
 *     rail's width instead of hanging off its left edge. Above 4, rows wrap and
 *     the leftover of the last row stays panel-toned, which keeps the rail one
 *     solid object rather than a broken grid.
 *
 * R2 — NOTHING IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE. content.credentials has no derived
 * fallback at all (astro-builder passes `c.credentials ?? undefined`, and
 * derive-content-defaults never invents a credential), so this band exists only
 * where an owner put something on record. Consequences, all deliberate:
 *   · with no filled row the WHOLE <section> is omitted — no eyebrow, no
 *     headline, no rail, no padding (R5: the gate counts the ROWS, never the
 *     tag or the headline, which other blocks get filled for free).
 *   · title and body are guarded independently and OUTSIDE their tags, so a
 *     half-filled row prints only what was typed — never an empty <h3> holding
 *     open 9px of margin.
 *   · the head guard sits outside the wrapper div too, so an owner who typed
 *     neither field gets no empty box above the rail.
 *   · NO seal, shield, ribbon, stamp, tick or badge of any kind, and no logo
 *     strip. A drawn badge is a visual claim of certification that the owner
 *     never made; the design draws none either. The numeral and the hairline
 *     are the only ornament.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED (so none of it reads as an oversight):
 *   · no id and no onPage() anchor-remap helper: 'credentials' is not one of
 *     PageBK's PAGE_ANCHORS (about services gallery location faq main), nothing
 *     links here, and this section emits no href of any kind — a remap helper
 *     would be dead code with no href to remap.
 *   · no data-block marker: editorBridge only needs one when two prefixes share
 *     a <section> (the BJ case). This section is credentials.* alone, so the
 *     Blocks toggle resolves to it by hook weight already.
 *   · no `band-panel`: the design keeps CREDENTIALS on the page ground and puts
 *     the panel tone INSIDE the rail. Banding the section would erase the rail.
 *   · no button. The design gives this band no action; the contact button sits
 *     in SERVICES and in the CTA band.
 *   · no star row / rating ornament — the design draws those in TESTIMONIALS,
 *     and there is no rating on a credential to draw from.
 *
 * THREE DECISIONS A READER MIGHT QUESTION:
 *   1. VERTICAL RHYTHM is the wrapper's. The design writes `padding:100px 34px 0`
 *      on this section; PageBK's shared `section { padding: clamp(60px,8vw,100px) 0 }`
 *      is the contract for all 15 sections, so this file adds no section padding
 *      of its own. Changing it here alone would desynchronise this band from
 *      its siblings.
 *   2. THE HEADLINE MEASURE is RELEASED, not inherited. The design's CREDENTIALS
 *      h2 carries NO max-width and sits on one line at 1280px; the wrapper's
 *      `.h2 { max-width: 24ch }` would wrap it to two, so this file sets
 *      `max-width: none` — the same call ServicesBK (.s-h2) and GalleryBK make
 *      wherever the design draws no cap. Size, face, colour, the italic <em>
 *      and both mobile passes are still the wrapper's; only the cap is local.
 *   3. THE CELL DIVIDER is the design's `border-right` on every cell, and NOT a
 *      box-shadow. Grid items paint atomically in order-modified document order
 *      (CSS Grid §6.2 — they paint as inline-blocks), so a hairline cast to the
 *      right lands on the next cell's border box and is buried under that
 *      cell's opaque --night-2 ground, and one cast downwards is buried by the
 *      row below or clipped by the rail under the last row: a shadow-drawn
 *      divider renders nowhere. The trailing hairline on the last cell of a row
 *      is the design's own drawing and is reproduced, not tidied away — the
 *      same decision TrustBK records for the same idiom.
 *
 * R3 — one scoped <style>, no is:global. The headline is injected with set:html,
 * so scoped rules cannot reach an <em> inside it; its italic brass styling comes
 * from PageBK's global `:where(.h2 em)` rule and needs nothing here.
 *
 * CONTRAST on the rail's --night-2 ground (sRGB, computed):
 *   title  --bone-hi 15.6:1 · numeral --accent 6.29:1
 *   body   --muted   4.13:1 — BELOW AA, and it is the design's own value, on
 *   PageBK's accepted list. Lifting --muted is a wrapper-level, owner-level
 *   call; this file ships the design rather than silently re-deriving it.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBK passes it to every section uniformly.
// This band reads nothing outside content.*, so it is never destructured.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

// The schema declares 'credentials.items' with fallbackPaths ['credentials'],
// so the stored value is legitimately EITHER {tag, headline, items:[…]} or a
// bare array. Both shapes are read.
const cred = content.credentials || {};
// The eyebrow is credentials.tag ONLY. The design's "Credentials" literal is
// the designer filling in their own template; it is not this file's to type.
const tag = cred.tag || '';
const headline = cred.headline || '';

type Item = {
  title?: string; label?: string;
  body?: string; desc?: string; detail?: string; description?: string;
};
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(cred.items)
  ? cred.items
  : (Array.isArray(cred) ? cred : []);
const titleOf = (it: Item): string => it.title ?? it.label ?? '';
const bodyOf = (it: Item): string => it.body ?? it.desc ?? it.detail ?? it.description ?? '';

// R6 — a row the owner added but never filled would otherwise draw a cell, a
// numeral and 34px of padding over nothing. Drop the blanks (whitespace counts
// as blank) but carry the ORIGINAL index, so every data-field still writes to
// the real array slot: removing credential 2 must never redirect an edit into
// credential 3.
const rows = items
  .map((it, i: number) => ({ ...it, i }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(titleOf(r).trim() || bodyOf(r).trim()));

const hasHead = Boolean(tag || headline);
// The design's rail is four tracks. Below four rows we narrow to the row count
// so the rail stays a full-width object; above four, rows wrap into four.
const cols = Math.min(rows.length, 4);
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="cred">
    <div class="wrap">
      {hasHead && (
        <div class="cred-head reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="credentials.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2 cred-h" data-field="credentials.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        </div>
      )}
      <div class:list={['cred-rail', 'reveal', `cols-${cols}`, hasHead && 'has-head']} data-delay="1">
        {rows.map((it, k: number) => (
          <div class="cred-cell">
            {/* DISPLAY index — the rail reads 01, 02, 03 however many blank
                rows were dropped. No data-field: it is a counter, never stored.
                One literal aria-hidden, never a conditional one beside it. */}
            <span class="fig cred-n" aria-hidden="true">{String(k + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            {titleOf(it) && <h3 class="cred-t" data-field={`credentials.items.${it.i}.title`}>{titleOf(it)}</h3>}
            {bodyOf(it) && <p class="cred-p" data-field={`credentials.items.${it.i}.body`}>{bodyOf(it)}</p>}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}

<style>
  /* The section keeps PageBK's shared padding and the page ground: the design
     puts --night-2 inside the rail, not under the band. No band-panel here. */
  .cred { min-width: 0; }

  .cred-head { min-width: 0; }

  /* The design's headline margin is `.26em 0 40px`. The 40px moves onto the
     rail instead, so that a site with an eyebrow but no headline — or with
     neither — still gets the right gap and never a phantom one.
     max-width: none — the design's CREDENTIALS h2 carries NO measure cap and
     draws its ~30ch headline on one line at 1280px, which the wrapper's
     `.h2 { max-width: 24ch }` would break to two. Size, face, colour and the
     italic <em> still come from .h2; only the cap is released, the same call
     ServicesBK and GalleryBK make for the same reason. overflow-wrap rides with
     it so a pasted unbroken string still cannot widen the wrap above 760. */
  .cred-h { margin: .26em 0 0; max-width: none; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* ── THE RAIL — one hairline box, cells flush inside it, sharp 3px corners.
        Track counts are emitted as a class rather than a custom property so a
        breakpoint can override them: repeat() will not take a calc/min for its
        count. */
  .cred-rail {
    display: grid;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    background-color: var(--night-2);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .cred-rail.has-head { margin-top: 40px; }
  .cols-1 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .cols-2 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .cols-3 { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  .cols-4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); }

  .cred-cell {
    padding: 34px 26px;
    min-width: 0;
    background-color: var(--night-2);
    /* The design's own declaration, verbatim: a hairline on the RIGHT of every
       repeated cell.

       It is a BORDER and not a box-shadow. Grid items paint in order-modified
       document order, each atomically (CSS Grid §6.2 — they paint as
       inline-blocks), so an outer shadow cast to the right lands exactly on the
       next cell's border box and is buried under that cell's opaque --night-2
       background; a shadow cast downwards is buried the same way by the row
       below, and under the LAST row it falls outside the rail's padding box and
       is clipped by overflow:hidden. A shadow-drawn divider therefore renders
       nowhere at all. A border is part of the cell's own box and cannot be
       covered by a sibling.

       The trailing hairline on the last cell of a row is the design's drawing,
       not an oversight — its sc-for puts border-right on every cell inside this
       same hairline box — so it is reproduced rather than tidied into a
       border-left-except-first, which is a different drawing. TrustBK takes the
       identical decision for the identical idiom. */
    border-right: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  /* Only reachable when an owner puts MORE than the design's four credentials on
     record: the rows wrap, and each row after the first needs the horizontal
     hairline the design never had to draw. Wrapping at this width can only
     happen in a 4-track rail (below four rows the rail narrows to the row
     count), so n+5 is the whole rule here. Restated at each breakpoint below,
     because the track count changes under it. */
  .cred-cell:nth-child(n + 5) { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }

  /* Prata 22px brass, then 14px of air. .fig supplies the face, the colour and
     the tabular figures; this makes it a block at the design's size. */
  .cred-n { display: block; font-size: 22px; margin-bottom: 14px; }

  /* PageBK sets :where(h1,h2,h3,h4) to Prata 400 — these titles are Figtree 600
     tracked caps, so the face, weight and colour are all restated. Line height
     is 1.3 rather than the wrapper's 1.1: the design's titles sit on one line,
     where leading is invisible, but an owner's longer title must wrap without
     the cap-height of the two lines touching. */
  .cred-t {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 11px; line-height: 1.3; letter-spacing: .2em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--bone-hi); margin: 0 0 9px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* PageBK's `:where(p){margin:0 0 1em}` is (0,1,1); qualifying with the cell
     class puts this above it so the zero margin actually lands. */
  .cred-cell .cred-p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--muted);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── 900 · a 4-up rail of 11px caps over a 13px body is unreadable once the
        track drops under ~200px, so 3 and 4 columns fold to 2. 1 and 2 already
        fit and are left alone. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .cols-3, .cols-4 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    /* Two up: the interior vertical rule is now the odd cell's right border, and
       every cell from the third on gains a horizontal one. The design's
       34px 26px padding is untouched — a ~414px track is not pressing on it. */
    .cred-cell:nth-child(n + 3) { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  }

  /* ── 520 · one column, and nothing may push the page sideways: titles and
        bodies already break anywhere, and the rail itself is a grid of
        minmax(0,1fr) tracks so a long word cannot widen it. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .cred-rail { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    /* 40px under a clamp(27px,7.2vw,40px) headline is proportionally a third of
       the line; 30px keeps the design's ratio at phone scale. */
    .cred-rail.has-head { margin-top: 30px; }
    /* ONLY the VERTICAL padding moves off the design's 34px 26px, and only
       because stacking spends it once per row. The 26px HORIZONTAL is the
       design's and stays — the wrap is 92vw (~330px at 360), so nothing is
       pressing on it. The trailing right hairline is dropped here: one-up it
       would double the rail's own right border down the full height of the
       page instead of dividing anything. */
    .cred-cell { padding: 28px 26px; border-right: 0; }
    .cred-cell:nth-child(n + 2) { border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  }
</style>
